            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The squares of natural numbers are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "perfect squares.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " For example, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … are all perfect squares."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.144,
            0.68,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Patterns and Properties of Perfect Squares"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.175,
            0.847,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Find the squares of the first 30 natural numbers and fill in the table below."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.199,
            0.772,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.252,
                0.199,
                0.772,
                0.512
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_table_4.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>1<sup>2</sup> = 1</td><td>11<sup>2</sup> = 121</td><td>21<sup>2</sup> = 441</td></tr><tr><td>2<sup>2</sup> = 4</td><td>12<sup>2</sup> =</td><td>22<sup>2</sup>=</td></tr><tr><td>3<sup>2</sup> = 9</td><td>13<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4<sup>2</sup> = 16</td><td>14<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5<sup>2</sup> = 25</td><td>15<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6<sup>2</sup> =</td><td>16<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>7<sup>2</sup> =</td><td>17<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>8<sup>2</sup> =</td><td>18<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>9<sup>2</sup> =</td><td>19<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>10<sup>2</sup> =</td><td>20<sup>2</sup> =</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.518,
            0.801,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What patterns do you notice? Share your observations and make conjectures."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.508,
            0.904,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.816,
                0.508,
                0.904,
                0.564
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.562,
            0.847,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Study the squares in the table above. What are the digits in the units places of these numbers? All these numbers end with 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 or 9. None of them end with 2, 3, 7 or 8."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.621,
            0.712,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If a number ends in 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 or 9, is it always a square?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.601,
            0.904,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.817,
                0.601,
                0.904,
                0.659
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.646,
            0.849,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The numbers 16 and 36 are both squares with 6 in the units place. 1 However, 26, whose units digit is also 6, is not a square. Therefore, we cannot determine if a number is a square just by looking at the digit in the units place. But, the units digit can tell us when a number is not a square. If a number ends with 2, 3, 7, or 8, then we can definitely say that it is not a square."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.76,
            0.848,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Write 5 numbers such that you can determine by looking at their units digit that they are not squares."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.802,
            0.848,
            0.861
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The squares, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 ^ { 2 } , 9 ^ { 2 } , 1 1 ^ { 2 } , 1 9 ^ { 2 } , 2 1 ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 9 ^ { 2 } ;"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " all have 1 in their units place. Write the next two squares. Notice that if a number has 1 or 9 in the units place, then its square ends in 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.862,
            0.846,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Let us consider square numbers ending in 6: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 6 = 4 ^ { 2 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 6 \\: = \\: 6 ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 9 6 = 1 4 ^ { 2 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "256 = 16^{2}, 576 = 24^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "6 7 6 = 2 6 ^ { 2 }"
            }
          ]
        },
